The metadata referenced Net Zero Cloud objects that do not exist, so the
package could not deploy into any org — not just one missing the licence.

Objects:
  StnryAsstEnvrSrc            -> StnryAssetEnvrSrc
  StnryAsstCrbnFprint         -> StnryAssetCrbnFtprnt
  StnryAsstFossilFuelEnrgyUse -> StnryAssetEnrgyUse
  SpplrMtrc                   -> no such object; use Supplier
  VehicleAsset                -> no such object; use VehicleAssetEnrgyUse

Fields:
  Stage                             -> FootprintStage
  TotalScope1Emissions              -> TotScope1EmissionsInTco2e
  TotalScope2LocationBasedEmissions -> TotScope2LocBasedEmissions
  TotalScope2MarketBasedEmissions   -> TotScope2MktBasedEmissions
  RecordType                        -> StationaryAssetType
  TotalCarbonFootprint, Scope1Emissions, Scope2Emissions,
  Scope3Emissions                   -> no equivalent, removed

Two modelling consequences. Emissions by Reporting Year no longer shows a
combined total: location-based and market-based Scope 2 are alternative
measures of the same emissions, so adding both to Scope 1 double counts,
and there is no TotalCarbonFootprint field to fall back on. And fleet fuel
now comes from VehicleAssetEnrgyUse, since VehicleAssetEmssnSrc carries
VehicleType but no fuel field.

Renames follow: Accounts_with_or_without_Supplier_Metrics ->
Accounts_with_or_without_Suppliers, Standalone_Vehicle_Assets ->
Standalone_Vehicle_Energy_Uses, Suppliers_Missing_Metrics ->
Accounts_Not_Registered_As_Suppliers, Fleet_by_Fuel_Type ->
Fleet_Fuel_by_Fuel_Type.

Verified against the Net Zero Cloud Developer Guide object reference for
API 67.0 (Summer '26). The three child relationship names used in the
report type joins are NOT documented by Salesforce and follow convention;
each carries a VERIFY IN ORG comment and the README explains how to read
the real names from a describe.

README also documents which org types actually include Net Zero Cloud
(a plain Developer Edition org and a standard Trailhead Playground do
not), and what the deploy button actually does.

